Seems to be a fad.  I started doing it today.  

Basically, Puddings, like most other creatures, will drop random items
when they die, even if they didn't have items in their inventory, and
even if they were spawned by division and not created independently. 

If you hit a pudding with a metal weapon, and the pudding isn't killed,
it divides.   There is no limit to how many P's can divide from a single
mother.  So you sit there in your fireproof cloak with your -6
thoroughly corroded weapon in which you aren't skilled, and whack them
and maybe heal them until they literally fill the level that you have
prepared by digging out every wall.  Then you switch to your blessed +7 
rustproof artifact weapon and basically swim through the level killing
them as you please.  Every once in a while, one drops an item.  Less
frequently, one drops a really useful item.  Now and then you can get
one to die on your altar and sacrifice it.  Or eat one.  You get the
idea.  

I wondered what all the fuss was about until I tried it.  It would make
it pretty easy to go wishless, since if you have the patience, you can
get pretty much anything you'd like out of a pudding farm.  I've seen
them drop wands of polymorph and countless scrolls and potions.  On
balance, it's so profitable that it makes me feel dirty, and I'm
actually looking forward to the next version which will no doubt curb
this advantage for the patient hero.

Seriously, the next time one drops a scroll of genocide, I'm probably
going to put P's out of their misery.

I'd "fix" this by making P's divide so that every once in a while, a
green slime is generated (it would have to ignore geno and extinction
too).  I don't think it would be nearly as easy then.   What I expect to
happen is monsters won't have death-drop if they were spawned from
another monster (I suppose this would apply to gremlins too).  Still you
could get experience and score points from them, but it's really the
huge (and I do mean, huge) number of items you get from the technique
that makes it interesting.  Think, floor lists that go on for hundreds
of pages.  Thousands of lock picks, maybe one ice box.  Get it?

See, the puddings can't hurt you if you are pretty buff and erodeproof, 
and not only that, there are full swarms of them so they make a  buffer
between you and any other monster.  In my game, L's and MMF's are gone,
and nothing else that can do damage from a distance has been generated
up here.  (My farm is on DL5).  



